From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Copilot <copilot@github.com>
Date: Sat, 07 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: Enable clock output after controller reset in probe

After sunxi_mmc_probe() issues SUNXI_MMC_GCTRL_RESET, all controller
registers (including CLKCR) are reset to their hardware defaults.
CLKCR.CLK_ENABLE resets to 0, disabling clock output to the card.

The subsequent mmc_start_init() call goes through mmc_set_initial_state()
which calls mmc_set_clock(mmc, 0, MMC_CLK_ENABLE). Because mmc->clock is
0, sunxi_mmc_set_ios_common() skips the mmc_config_clock() call entirely,
leaving CLKCR with CLK_ENABLE = 0.

Without the clock enabled, the hardware cannot generate the 80 init CLK
pulses required for CMD0 (SEND_INIT_SEQ), and no subsequent commands
ever reach the card. This manifests as mmc_init() failing silently for
the eMMC (mmc@1c11000) when booting from eMMC on Cubieboard4 / sun9i A80.

Fix this by explicitly setting CLK_ENABLE in CLKCR and calling
mmc_update_clk() immediately after the controller reset in the DM probe
path. This ensures the card clock is running at the module clock rate
(24 MHz) before the MMC core starts the card initialization sequence.

The module clock itself (CCU sd2_clk_cfg) is unaffected by the
controller reset, so it retains the 24 MHz configuration written by the
preceding mmc_set_mod_clk() call.

Signed-off-by: Copilot <copilot@github.com>
---
 drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
index 3333333..4444444 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
@@ -688,7 +688,19 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_probe(struct udevice *dev)
 	/* Reset controller */
 	writel(SUNXI_MMC_GCTRL_RESET, &priv->reg->gctrl);
 	udelay(1000);
 
+	/*
+	 * Enable clock output to the card after the controller reset.
+	 * mmc_set_initial_state() sets mmc->clock = 0, which causes
+	 * sunxi_mmc_set_ios_common() to skip mmc_config_clock(), leaving
+	 * CLKCR with its reset value (CLK_ENABLE = 0). Without the clock
+	 * enabled, the hardware cannot generate the 80 init CLK pulses for
+	 * CMD0 (SEND_INIT_SEQ) and subsequent commands never reach the card.
+	 */
+	setbits_le32(&priv->reg->clkcr, SUNXI_MMC_CLK_ENABLE);
+	mmc_update_clk(priv);
+
 	return 0;
 }
